em or  our role on the system I gave him a  civics lecture talking to the recorder  naturally I said I'm afraid we're gonna  have to send you home we can't use your  affidavit but thanks for coming by and  we threw him out what are the other  if--and I found out years later somebody  had comic  it accidentally filed in another case  report that DEA agents were surveying my  office that was standing outside my  office following me around to see what I  do in it I was being investigated by  these thugs and they had gotten the  wrong guy shows you this is government  work right that somebody walked out of  my office building on Broad Street  Boston and they followed him thinking it  was me it wasn't me  they follow him down to what was then  the elevated expressway there was a car  parked under the elevated expressway the  guy gets in and has a sexual encounter  with a young lady who was behind the  steering wheel and they're all excited  they go over to the window they knocked  on the window the guy is horrified it  turns out of course he's got a wife and  kids at home right and they they said  you're Harvey silverglade aren't you and  what does the guy supposed to say oh no  no I'll show you my driver's license I'm  Joe Smith I married my wife lives in  Malden so the guy says oh yeah I'm  Harvey so goodnight so they're all  excited they've now caught me in  flagrante delicto so to speak and this  they made and only later did they  realize they had the wrong guy I wasn't  even around I was in town that day talk  a little bit about your kind of  intellectual genealogy or journey you  you know why it was civil libertarianism  something that you came out of the womb  believing in or what what brought you to  your set of beliefs I had a kind of  typical middle-class Jewish upbringing  and a half Jewish neighborhood in  Brooklyn and then my father who was a  furrier at the time was having trouble  with the fur Union they were sending  thugs around they didn't like the fact  that he reported a crooked election so  we moved to New Jersey where he was able  to get away from the furriers Union and  New Jersey suburbs suburb of Hackensack  I did not like the suburbs I was a  Brooklyn boy and I left from there and  went on to college and Princeton because  I had a big scholarship  Princeton was just beginning to open up  to two Jewish kids and with no money I  got a full scholarship and my parents  and my grandparents of course told me I  was gonna go to medical school and be a  nice Jewish doctor just like you know  that's what they came over from Russia  that's what they escaped from Russia and  Poland for so I could be a doctor right  that's a heavy burden and then the end  of my sophomore year for that summer for  the first time I left the territory of  the United States and I went to abroad  they got me a job Princeton got me a job  paid for my transportation that work in  Paris and it was my first time out of  the country I was away from family for  three blessed months that summer and I  rethought my entire life I came back  deciding that the things that people do  to each other much more bothersome to me  than the things the germs do to people  and I decided I was going to go into  journalism and I was gonna go to law  school so that I could understand the  legal system better sites when I started  out Law School with journalists the  career in mind ran into Alan Dershowitz  it was his first year at Harvard Law  School  my first year we became friends and by  the third year he convinced me I should  try to I would work take the bar exam  work in a legal office for a couple  years to see whether I wanted to  actually be a criminal lawyer or a crime  reporter and I went to both III got out  I got a job with a very interesting  little firm that did criminal defense  work I did some First Amendment work and  I decided that I would be a little bit  of both I would be a practitioner and I  would write about it and there were very  few legally trained people those days  who wrote that was one of the first  people I wrote for the Boston Phoenix a  you know a countercultural newspaper  there actually wrote that the  my criticism of judges lawyers it was  unheard of to criticize a judge in print  the assumption was that you would lose  your you know they wouldn't like you you  wouldn't do as well on cases quite the  opposite suddenly I got treated very  well I got treated fairly  they weren't friendly but I got treated  fairly because if they really did  terrible things I would write about it  you know and so fear is a tremendous  power and they don't like you know  sunlight is the best disinfectant it's  true who are your legal heroes  Alex Kaczynski in the Ninth Circuit  there's somebody who he doesn't care if  people like him or not he tells tells  the truth he writes legal opinions he  holds prosecutors feet to the fire you  know naturally I thought Clarence Darrow  was a somewhat of a role model Alan  Dershowitz was a great influence we did  a lot of cases together and for a while  I was ahead of him because I had more  experience but Luke was a brilliant  strategist and an appellate lawyer and  so woman I've had some law partners  who've had a lot of guts and that's  really the people who make the system  work though it's yeah the people who  make the system work are the people who  are not afraid to say you know the old  truth to power who aren't a lot who  aren't afraid to say when the judge or a  prosecutor or an FBI agent totally out  of hand just as an endpoint you are  writing a sequel to three felonies a day  what is the title and what do you hope  to accomplish with that book and when  will we see it on our shelf well three  felonies a day remember was a book that  talked about quirks in the system that  that easily easily could result in the  conviction of innocent people  I had represented enough people where  they had been convicted and sentenced to  long prison terms for not doing anything  that I would consider criminal just as  an ordinary reasonably Oakland  human  and it had a tremendous influence I was  actually surprised lawyers were getting  to touch with me defendants people who  were in prison who had cases that were  equally as outrageous as anything I  wrote about and then I was getting kind  of confidential communications from  judges that told me that the system was  really broken and even judges some  judges understood it a lot of them  dramatically played along and some of  them cynically played along  but everybody realized that the system  was broken and I even heard believe it  or not from a few federal prosecutors  the condition that I never mentioned  that they contacted me and I'm not about  to mention you know I was fired  next question band it couldn't be take  this left or right because I talked  about victims on all ends of the  political spectrum and you know  businessman and artists and and some  quite ordinary ordinary people and so  Roger Kimball was said to me about two  years ago you know Harvey everybody  tells me that your book is brilliant in  setting out the problem and totally  deficient because you don't suggest the  solutions and I said well when I wrote  read the wrote the book I wasn't sure  what the solutions were and besides you  told me I already had a manuscript that  was twice as long as it could be is it I  want you to write a follow-up to the  book that suggests solutions and I want  you to make it simple enough so that  legislators can understand it that's a  challenge legislators could understand  that everybody can understand it and I'm  hoping to have a manuscript this  December to my publisher it'll come out  sometime next year it's called  conviction machine how did this man  delay and Errol Morris the documentary  filmmaker working with me try to put  together a series of shorts illustrating  the problem that the system has both  state and federal mostly a federal  problem of the absence of what we call  mens rea requirement  and statutes we've been talking with  Harvey silverglade he's the co-author of  the shadow University co-founder a fire  author of three felonies a day and he of  the forthcoming conviction machine how  to dismantle it  Harvey thanks so much for talking with  us and the work you do my pleasure thank  you for reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie  you
 

